          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "How long does a common cold usually last?",
          "answer": "A common cold usually lasts **about 7 to 10 days**. Symptoms are usually worst around days 2 to 3, then ease. A cough often lasts 2 to 3 weeks, which is normal. Yellow or green mucus is also part of the normal course and does not by itself mean a bacterial infection. (Source: *Upper respiratory infection (common cold)*, general clinical reference; *Upper respiratory infection: antibiotic stewardship*, guidance summary.)\n\n**Get emergency help right away** if you have any of these, as listed in the red-flag guidance:\n- Breathlessness at rest, or trouble speaking in full sentences\n- Blue or gray lips, tongue, face or fingertips\n- Confusion, unusual drowsiness, or difficulty waking\n- A rash of spots that does not fade when pressed with a glass\n- Fever with a severe headache and a stiff neck\n- Very fast breathing, dizziness on standing, or not passing urine for many hours\n\n(Sources: *Severe difficulty breathing*; *Sepsis*; *Signs suggesting meningitis*.)\n\n**Talk to a clinician** if your symptoms are not improving after about 10 days, or if they get better and then clearly worsen again. The antibiotic guidance lists symptoms lasting 10 days or more without improvement as one pattern that may need review. It does not mean you have a bacterial infection, only that it is worth having someone assess you.\n\nI can't say from this information whether your own symptoms fit this pattern. A clinician can check that.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
